	<section label="5. How it looks">
		<icon slot="icon" name="palette"/>
		<list bulleted>
			<item>Neutral: no colour and no fill of its own (decision Q20). <text code>Net effect</> is bold, in the text's own size and ink.</>
			<item><text code>recommended</> is grey (muted), as everywhere.</>
			<item>Its list sits close under the label.</>
			<item>It doesn't fold: the item around it does.</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="6. Written by">
		<icon slot="icon" name="terminal"/>
		<list bulleted>
			<item>Claude writes it inside the HTML it passes to <text code>epic</> (<text code>add ... --details</>, <text code>decide --details</>, <text code>details --file</>, <text code>updated</>): no command makes one by itself.</>
			<item>The epic tool turns the OLD hand-shaped markup into this element, so a doc never holds the old shape. <list bulleted><item>It does this in any HTML a command takes: <text code>add --details</>, <text code>decide --details</>, <text code>details --file</>, <text code>updated</>.</> <item>The code that does it: <text code>IncomingHtml</> and <text code>ProseRewrite</>.</> <item>Prose edited by hand in a part file isn't passed through it.</> <item>The old shape: <text code>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Net effect (A):&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</> and its list.</></></>
			<item>The converter did the same to every converted doc (<text code>ProseUpgrader</>).</>
			<item>A label worded otherwise stays plain prose: <text code>&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Net effect (once fixed):&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</> and its list.</>
			<item>On a details page it goes as plain prose: <text code>Net effect (A):</> over its list (<text code>PlanItem.asProse()</>).</>
		</>
	</>
